  "interpretation": [
    "Ordinary free-fit backfill is active and improves the native P95 gap; it does not cover the funded policy tail reduction in this run. Keep ordinary as the strong simple comparator.",
    "The funded policy reduces rare large output gaps with 102 actual preemptions (59 forced), versus 59 ordinary and 43 native; this is a service-tail result, not a fewer-preemptions result.",
    "Fund versus ordinary costs 1.524% actual output rate and 1.850% mean flow while lowering the request-max-gap P95 by 59.464%. Median gap and many individual request delays rise, so this is not distribution-wide domination.",
    "The funded frontier loses at the 1-second gap bound for TTFT bounds 20, 30 and 40 seconds, and improves at all four 2-second-gap points. A primary pause-tail objective must retain TTFT, flow and strict-gap costs.",
    "The two harmed examples are observed request outcomes across independent scheduling trajectories; their raw preemption counts and gap endpoints do not identify a unique causal recovery action.",
    "Article generation remains overwhelmingly capped and generated sequences differ. The native 0044036 request emits 173 tokens and stops, whereas ordinary and fund emit 1024 and hit the cap; its +13.14-second flow difference cannot be interpreted as an equal-work scheduling penalty.",
    "This run motivates testing whether recovery quantum controls the remaining moderate gaps and displaced-request cost, but supplies no evidence yet that a new quantum policy beats the existing funded Q1 path."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "All three paths use the same already-seen input but have independent scheduling trajectories.",
    "Ordinary free-fit backfill is a strong simple baseline action, not a complete reproduction of CacheOPT or UniBoost.",
    "Action events within one run are not independent replicates; all displaced-request costs remain in the full-cohort metrics.",
    "This challenge is neither fresh confirmation nor a novelty claim.",
    "No semantic quality evaluation for the article workload; the natural-EOS healthy task run is a separate result.",
    "One observed run per arm gives no run-level uncertainty estimate."
  ]
}
